an we possibly can  we either provide the financial support  or the actual sport to be able to  provide that and i think so far okay you  know we're leading the world in that we  will continue to do so and but it's a as  we've seen  overnight  rather sort of graphically this is a  very fast-changing situation so we are  very much uh watching this on an  hour-by-hour basis okay odessa is a port  that is incredibly important to ukraine  of course there are all sorts of reasons  they're asking for um anti-ship missiles  sunny times reporting yesterday the  prime minister wants to provide them  with that how far along the line are we  with that  well i think we've heard what the pm  said yesterday uh  clearly something we're considering yeah  absolutely i mean he said so he wants to  be able to help as far as that's  concerned uh and and those these are the  judgments which he and others are making  but it's not this is not just the uk  this is about leading a world response  if the response is to be effective it  needs uh the west and many many nations  to come together in that common endeavor  so it is better that we make sure that  we take everybody with us that's what  we're trying to and that is indeed what  president zielinski has praised the uk  for doing which is actually leading this  global  effort okay  we've seen people  with their hands tied behind their back  shot in the head not once not twice many  many many times  quite rightly people are fleeing that 11  000 people want to come or have applied  to come to wales from ukraine will 11  000 people be allowed to come i mean  we and welsh government this is one of  those areas where there is more than one  um  sort of responsible body are determined  uh to live up to our reputation of being  a nation of sanctuary we want to make it  as easy as possible we want to make it  as quick as possible that do need to be  inevitably they do need to be  checks of some i hope limited nature  just to make sure that everybody's safe  in that process but absolutely there  should be no you know  those images that you've just described  ought to remind anybody or everybody  who's looking at this and we haven't got  the time to uh to sort of you know  naval gaze over this we have to get on  with it i think we are doing a i think a  a reasonable job we can always do more  we can always go further we can always  go faster but  letting people in the country so far my  question is will the eleven thousand  ukrainians ten thousand ukrainians who  want to come to  wales be allowed to come yeah i hope so  it's a matter of welsh government as i  say but uh we're working with them very  closely we want that to happen  there shouldn't in a sense be any uh  limits to this we should be responding  to need so why is it taking so long well  this is always i  i would love to be able to i would love  to be able to come here i would love to  be able to come here and and and say  that we can just flick our things and  everything would happen in states i  think most people rather are sometimes  some practical ones who are trying to  keep the bureaucracy to an absolute  minimum  and welsh government let me just say  minister this it's 38 days since ukraine  went to war we know what the russians  are capable of given what happened  in syria we knew even before they went  to war that it was likely that's what  the foreign secretary sat there and told  me time after time so why have we still  only allowed a handful of ukrainians  into the country i think that's  i think that's a bit harsh right we've  already approved 25 000 uh visas i don't  think that's happening  to me that that doesn't seem to be a  handful i think it's we're working we're  working with trying to make sure that as  far as my area of responsibility uh is  wales is as open and available  immediately uh that's already there are  families arriving well we want more we  want to respond to uh to demand and  respond to need cause as i say by the  images that we've seen we don't want to  we're not here to stand in the way of  any of that and just  sit there with a clipboard saying no we  actually want to move heaven and earth  to make sure that we get people into  wales safely  for as long as they need to stay in  order to be already to be safeguarded  how many families are already in wales  well we've committed at the moment uh  we've we've we've got an open-ended  commitment so how many we've got  that's a question you have to ask welsh  government no but you did say that  they've already arrived yeah they have  there are families arriving there are  families in my own constituency i can't  tell you how many across wales this is  an ever-changing picture um and it's a  matter of welsh government to answer  that specific question all i'm saying um  is that we are literally trying to make  sure that everybody who needs to come  everybody who wants to come  can come because  as i said there is no reason there is no  reason at all why we would want to slow  why would we want to switch let's move  on and talk about kobe do if we make  because we have a limited amount of time  this morning um  we're just reading in the papers this  morning that  the office for national statistics i  think it was offstead forgive me saying  that a generation of babies and toddlers  struggling to crawl and communicate as a  result of lockdowns uh enforced by this  government how do you think  history will judge this government when  it comes to young people i think the uh  the covert inquiry will do a number of  things as far as i'm concerned the  sooner it concludes better so i think  what it will show is that some of the  decisions which have been taken over the  last couple of years which people have  been highly critical of have actually  been very sensible decisions in the very  rapidly changing circumstances where we  were learning as we went along and i  think that when we look back and did we  go into lockdown too soon too late what  were the considerations what was the  effect on kids education and life  chances i think that is where we will  learn some uh um  very frank lessons as far as i'm  concerned i think that both welsh  government in my case and or in the case  of wells and uk government in the wider  context i think actually will be judged  better than sometimes uh people give  them credit for because we reacted very  fast to a very rapidly changing  situation we did everything we could at  the time to protect the economy as well  as protecting public health some of  those decisions would have been uh  correct some of those decisions you know  when we look back over two or three  years we may have made differently i  think all of them whether you're mark  drakeford sat in cardiff or whether  you're  you're me sat here uh in westminster all  of those decisions were taken with  public health uh in mind and the  protection of public health if we didn't  get them all right yeah i think the  inquiry  will reveal that  wait for the inquiry um as far as  partygate is concerned of course the  people in charge of those decisions that  you've been illustrating there have  started to receive fines um including  helen mcnamara deputy cabinet secretary  then deputy cabinet secretary  um who was basically in charge of ethics  for the government at the time she had a  karaoke machine in her office it was  used at one of these parties she's  received a fine  karaoke at downing street at the same  time that people were being told they  couldn't sing in church does that sit  comfortably with you no of course it  doesn't sit comfortably with anybody um  and the pm has on numerous occasions  come out and said um how bit how  bitterly he regrets uh the circumstances  which led up to the most of course it  doesn't i don't barely a day goes by  when i don't look back and wish i did  something different when i look back  over two years and look at every  decision i made every judgement i made i  